Here’s a single‑file HTML page that does exactly what you asked for: a button that, when clicked, shows a random joke from a list of four jokes.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<title>Random Joke Generator</title>
<style>
  body {
    font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
    margin: 2rem;
    text-align: center;
  }
  #joke {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    color: #333;
    min-height: 2rem;   /* keep space even when empty */
  }
  button {
    padding: 0.6rem 1.2rem;
    font-size: 1rem;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Click the button for a joke!</h1>
<button id="jokeBtn">Tell me a joke</button>

<div id="joke"></div>

<script>
  // Array of jokes
  const jokes = [
    "Why don’t scientists trust atoms? Because they make up everything!",
    "I told my wife she was drawing her eyebrows too high. She looked surprised.",
    "Why did the scarecrow win an award? Because he was outstanding in his field!",
    "I’m reading a book on anti‑gravity. It’s impossible to put down!"
  ];

  // Grab the button and the joke container
  const btn = document.getElementById('jokeBtn');
  const jokeDiv = document.getElementById('joke');

  // Function to pick a random joke
  function showRandomJoke() {
    const randomIndex = Math.floor(Math.random() * jokes.length);
    jokeDiv.textContent = jokes[randomIndex];
  }

  // Attach click handler
  btn.addEventListener('click', showRandomJoke);
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

### How it works
1. **HTML** – A button (`#jokeBtn`) and a `<div>` (`#joke`) to display the joke.
2. **CSS** – Very light styling to center the content and make the button look nice.
3. **JavaScript** –  
   * An array `jokes` holds the four jokes.  
   * `showRandomJoke()` picks a random index and updates the text of the joke `<div>`.  
   * The button’s `click` event is wired to that function.

Save the code as `index.html` and open it in any browser. Each click will show a new random joke. Enjoy!

**Solution Idea**

The only “extra” data we are allowed to use is the two input arrays themselves.  
We can therefore

1. **Sort** both arrays *in place* (e.g. `std::sort` in C++).  
   Sorting does not create a new container – it only rearranges the existing
   elements.

2. Use two indices (`i` for the first array, `j` for the second) and walk
   through the sorted arrays simultaneously – the classic *two‑pointer*
   technique.

   * If `a[i] == b[j]` we found a common element → print it and advance both
     pointers.
   * If `a[i] < b[j]` the smaller element cannot match anything later in
     `b`, so advance `i`.
   * Otherwise advance `j`.

The algorithm runs in  
`O(n log n + m log m)` time (dominated by the two sorts) and uses only
`O(1)` additional memory.

---

### C++17 Implementation

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n, m;
    if (!(cin >> n >> m)) return 0;          // read sizes

    vector<int> a(n), b(m);
    for (int &x : a) cin >> x;              // read first array
    for (int &x : b) cin >> x;              // read second array

    sort(a.begin(), a.end());               // in‑place sort
    sort(b.begin(), b.end());

    int i = 0, j = 0;
    bool found = false;
    while (i < n && j < m) {
        if (a[i] == b[j]) {
            if (!found) {                   // first common element
                cout << a[i];
                found = true;
            } else {
                cout << ' ' << a[i];
            }
            ++i; ++j;
        } else if (a[i] < b[j]) {
            ++i;
        } else {
            ++j;
        }
    }

    if (!found) cout << "No common elements";
    cout << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

#### How to run

```
g++ -std=c++17 -O2 -pipe -static -s -o common common.cpp
./common
```

**Input format**

```
n m
a1 a2 ... an
b1 b2 ... bm
```

**Example**

```
Input:
5 6
1 5 3 9 7
4 3 8 1 9 10

Output:
1 3 9
```

The program prints all common elements in ascending order, separated by a
single space. If there are none, it prints `No common elements`. No
additional data structures beyond the two input arrays are used.

